  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nThis afternoon through Sunday night...\n\nAnother day of dangerous heat is in the forecast for much of the \narea this afternoon. Afternoon highs will peak into the mid to upper \n90s across much of our CWA. A few locations north of Hwy 82 could \nbriefly see daytime highs reach 100 degrees. The combination of \ndaytime highs in the 90s and dewpoints in the mid 70s will all for \nheat index reading values to peak in the 105-110 degree range across \nour CWA today. At this time no changes have been made to the heat \ngraphic, and the \"Elevated\" risk for dangerous heat will continue to \nbe advertised for much of our CWA for today. Likewise, the Heat \nAdvisory will remain in effect for western/northern parts of the \narea until later this evening. Isolated to scattered diurnal showers \nand storms will be possible this afternoon mainly for areas along \nand south of I-20. A few strong storms with wind gusts up to 40 mph \ncannot be ruled out. \n\nRain chances will begin to decrease later this evening leading to \nquiet weather conditions across our CWA heading into the overnight \nperiod. Areas south of I-20 will start to see cloud cover begin to \nincrease from west to east shortly after midnight with mostly clear \nskies elsewhere. Nighttime lows will dip into the low to mid 70s \nareawide. Given the clear skies, light sfc wind, and a humid \nboundary layer, some dense fog cannot be ruled out for central and \nsouthern portions of the area late tonight through early Sunday \nmorning. For this reason, a \"Limited\" risk for patchy dense fog has \nbeen introduced mainly for southern portions of the forecast area, \nincluding portions of northeast LA.\n\nLooking ahead into Sunday, a quick amplification of the upper level \ntough over the eastern CONUS will send a frontal boundary toward our \nCWA. There will be some added compressional heating ahead of the \nboundary with H850 temperatures in the low 20s deg C, and this will \nresult in continued elevated dangerous heat levels. Because of this \nan Heat Advisory has been introduced for our entire area from 11 am \nSunday morning until 7 pm Sunday evening.  In addition, a marginal \nsevere risk (Level 1 out of 5) will continue to be advertised for a \ngood portion of our CWA Sunday afternoon/evening as scattered storms \nshould initiate ahead of the front in an airmass characterized by \nsteep low to mid level lapse rates. This will support severe wind \ngusts in the more intense storms. If storm development becomes more \nimminent, we could certainly see the risk level increase to slight \n(2 of 5) as there is recent precedence for storms to overachieve \nwith the hotter days and anomalously hot H850 temps. Timing of the \nsevere storms for Sunday still looks to be from to 2 pm Sunday \nafternoon until 9 pm Sunday night. /CR/\n\nMonday through next Saturday...\n\nThe forecast messaging for the long term period remains the same \nwith no major modifications made to the overall forecast. Long term \nguidance continues to show a stubborn subtropical ridge that will \nunfortunately remain in place of the Gulf Coast States as we head \ninto the new work week, so  don't expect signs for early fall \nweather to show up any time soon. Instead, we can expected \nadditional limited to elevated dangerous heat threats and reduced \nrainfall coverage mostly limited to southern portions of the area. \nRain will start to increase in coverage across eastern and southern \nportions of the area by the weekend. /CR/",
